---
scope: src/service/parseBrainAide
description: >
  Single canonical reader for `.aide/config/brain.aide` — parses the two-field
  YAML frontmatter (`name` + `mcpServerConfig`) and extracts the six required
  body sections delimited by paired HTML-comment markers
  (`<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` / `-end`,
  `<!-- aide-config-start -->` / `-end`,
  `<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->` / `-end`,
  `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` / `-end`,
  `<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->` / `-end`,
  `<!-- aide-research-index-start -->` / `-end`, lowercase, case-sensitive,
  in required order) as separately-named fields, optionally applies
  install-time frontmatter-value interpolation to `mcpServerConfig.args` for
  advanced users (a surface the default scaffold does not exercise), and
  returns a structured value whose consumers each pull only the section they
  need: the runtime brain tool reads `orientation` and `config` live, the
  brain provisioner reads `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
  and `researchIndex` once at install time to scaffold their on-disk
  artifacts, and the sync verb reads only the frontmatter.
  `mcpServerConfig.args` is parsed as `(string | null)[]` — every element is
  either a fully-formed argument string OR YAML null, with null at any index
  serving as the explicit unwired-slot signal. The parser preserves null
  entries verbatim through to consumers; downstream consumers (sync,
  buildBrainState, the install-time interpolation helper) decide what to do
  with nulls per their own contracts. The parser does not coerce, validate,
  or reject null entries — null is part of the args type contract, and there
  is no literal-string sentinel (`<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, magic UUIDs,
  etc.) in this contract anywhere.
intent: >
  parseBrainAide is the single canonical-code module that knows the
  `brain.aide` file format. The file's body carries six named sections
  delimited by paired HTML-comment markers, partitioned by who reads them
  and when. Two are agent-facing live sections read by the runtime
  `aide_brain` tool whenever the agent calls it: the `orientation` section
  (the agent's runtime briefing — what the brain is, what tools to call to
  read it, the entry-point artifacts it contains, and how those fit into
  the agent's workflow) and the `config` section (the integration-specific
  wiring flow `/aide:brain config` executes verbatim, owning the
  per-backend argument shape and the steps for editing `brain.aide` and
  syncing). Four are install-time seeds the brain provisioner reads once
  and writes into the brain as on-disk files, after which the brain owns
  those files and the body bytes go dormant: the `playbookIndex` section
  (seeds the coding-playbook entry-point artifact at
  `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`), the `studyPlaybook` section
  (seeds the playbook-navigation methodology at
  `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`), the `updatePlaybook` section
  (seeds the playbook-maintenance methodology at
  `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, sibling of study-playbook), and
  the `researchIndex` section (seeds the research entry-point artifact at
  `research/research.md`). The "index" suffix calls out that
  `playbookIndex` and `researchIndex` seed the *index* of their hub, not
  the hub content itself; `studyPlaybook` and `updatePlaybook` carry no
  suffix because they seed methodology files, not indexes. The parser is
  the load-bearing boundary that keeps the file format hand-written (the
  user owns it) while every downstream consumer sees the same typed
  structure with each body section addressable by name. None of those
  consumers reach into the raw file format directly; they ask this module
  for the parsed result and access named fields. The frontmatter schema
  is the minimum that still has real consumers — exactly `name` (a label)
  and `mcpServerConfig` (consumed by sync). Every removed field
  (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`) is rejected as
  malformed-frontmatter so users migrate cleanly rather than carrying
  dead fields forward. The body section vocabulary is the parser's
  enumerated truth: every required marker pair must be present and
  well-formed, marker layout violations are hard failures, and bytes
  outside any marker pair are silently ignored — the parser extracts the
  six named sections, it does not police the surrounding file.
  Substitution is partitioned by audience: frontmatter-value interpolation
  (`${fieldName}`) into `mcpServerConfig.args` is install-time work the
  parser performs on demand for sync-class consumers; the default scaffold
  does not exercise this surface (the path lives byte-inline in args, or
  the slot reserved for the path is the literal YAML null entry that the
  config flow later replaces), and the only frontmatter value resolvable
  today is `${name}`. The six body sections each return verbatim with
  zero substitution because their audiences (the agent reading
  `orientation` and `config`; the install scaffolder writing entry-point
  and methodology artifacts to disk) are not the `.mcp.json` writer.
  The parser produces a strongly-typed result whose `mcpServerConfig.args`
  field is `(string | null)[]`. That broadening is load-bearing: type
  safety throughout the rest of the stack — sync's null-refusal,
  buildBrainState's null-detection driving the unwired-slot warning, the
  install-time interpolation helper's null-passthrough — all assume the
  parser preserves null entries accurately, neither coercing them to
  empty strings nor rejecting them as malformed. YAML null at any index
  IS the unwired-slot signal in the contract; no string sentinel exists
  at any layer of the package.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - >-
      "Reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` from disk and returns a structured
      value with the typed frontmatter object — exactly `name` (string)
      and `mcpServerConfig` (object with `command: string` and
      `args: (string | null)[]`) — plus six separately-named body fields:
      `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
      `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex`, each carrying the verbatim
      bytes between its opening marker and its closing marker. No other
      frontmatter fields and no other body fields appear in the returned
      shape. Consumers reference shared types from `src/types/index.ts`,
      not module-internal definitions."
    - >-
      "`mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]`. Every element
      is either a fully-formed argument string OR YAML null — a bare `-`
      with no scalar after, which YAML 1.1/1.2 parses to JS `null`. The
      parser preserves null entries verbatim from YAML through to the
      typed result: no coercion to empty string, no substitution of any
      placeholder string, no skip, no compaction of the array, no
      reordering. Index positions in the parsed args match index
      positions in the source YAML one-to-one. Null is part of the
      contract — the explicit unwired-slot signal that downstream
      consumers (sync's null-refusal, buildBrainState's null-detection,
      the install-time interpolation helper's null-passthrough) all
      branch on by structure."
    - >-
      "The parser does not detect, recognize, or branch on any
      literal-string sentinel in `mcpServerConfig.args`. `<BRAIN_PATH>`,
      `<API_TOKEN>`, `__UNWIRED__`, magic UUIDs, angle-bracketed
      placeholders of any shape — none of these are part of the contract
      at the parse layer. Every string in args is just a string the
      parser passes through verbatim; the unwired-slot signal is
      structural (YAML null), not lexical. The parser sees only YAML
      structure; sentinel-aware logic does not exist at any layer of the
      package."
    - >-
      "Each consumer pulls only the body section it needs by name,
      partitioned cleanly between live agent-facing reads and one-shot
      install-time seeds. The runtime brain tool reads `orientation` and
      `config` live whenever the agent calls `aide_brain` (orientation by
      default, config when `kind` selects it); the brain provisioner
      reads `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and
      `researchIndex` once at install time to scaffold the four
      corresponding on-disk files in the brain (the coding-playbook
      entry-point at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, the
      study-playbook methodology at `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
      the update-playbook methodology at
      `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, and the research entry-point
      at `research/research.md`); the sync verb and boot reporter read
      frontmatter only. The runtime brain tool does NOT read
      `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, or
      `researchIndex` — the `study-playbook` and `update-playbook` skills
      and the playbook/research index navigation all reach their content
      through the on-disk artifacts the provisioner scaffolds, not
      through the brain.aide body sections directly. No consumer
      re-extracts a section from a freeform body string, and no consumer
      reads a section it does not own — the parser hands each consumer
      exactly the typed slice that consumer is contracted to use."
    - >-
      "All six body sections return verbatim — byte-identical to what
      the user wrote between each opening marker and its matching closing
      marker. No trimming, no normalization, no markdown rewriting, no
      template engine, on any of `orientation`, `config`,
      `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, or
      `researchIndex`."
    - >-
      "Body schema is exactly six named sections delimited by paired
      HTML-comment markers, recognized lowercase and case-sensitive, in
      required order: the `<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-orientation-end -->` pair first, then the
      `<!-- aide-config-start -->` / `<!-- aide-config-end -->` pair,
      then the `<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->` pair, then the
      `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` pair, then the
      `<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->` pair, then the
      `<!-- aide-research-index-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-research-index-end -->` pair — twelve markers total, six
      pairs, distinct open/close tokens per section. The first two pairs
      (`orientation`, `config`) are agent-facing live sections; the next
      four (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
      `researchIndex`) are install-time seeds, ordered by destination
      directory (`coding-playbook/*` first, then `research/*`). The
      convention extends the existing `writeMethodology` HTML-comment
      marker precedent."
    - >-
      "Marker layout violations are hard failures returning
      `malformed-body` with a reason naming the violating marker. The
      violation set: a required pair missing (any of the six), a marker
      token malformed or typo'd, a closing marker without a prior
      matching opener, an opener without a closer, the six pairs
      appearing out of the required order, and any nesting of markers
      inside another marker pair. Any such violation halts the pipeline;
      the parser does not attempt recovery."
    - >-
      "Bytes outside any marker pair — between the frontmatter and the
      first opener, between any closer and the next opener, after the
      last closer — are silently ignored by the parser. The parser's job
      is to extract the six named sections, not to police the rest of
      the file; users may keep notes-to-self, scratch text, or comments
      in those regions without warning or error."
    - >-
      "Strict-failure migration covers every pre-rework shape. A
      `brain.aide` using the original heading-based body schema
      (`## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub` with no marker
      pairs present) returns `malformed-body` with a reason naming the
      expected new markers. A `brain.aide` carrying the prior three-pair
      marker-bounded body (`aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`,
      `aide-research-*` only) returns `malformed-body` with a reason
      naming every expected new marker. A `brain.aide` carrying the
      prior four-pair marker-bounded body (`aide-prose-*`,
      `aide-playbook-*`, `aide-study-playbook-*`, `aide-research-*` —
      missing the new `<!-- aide-config-* -->` and
      `<!-- aide-update-playbook-* -->` pairs and using the retired
      `aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`, and `aide-research-*` names
      that are now `aide-orientation-*`, `aide-playbook-index-*`, and
      `aide-research-index-*`) returns `malformed-body` with a reason
      naming every expected new marker that is missing or
      retired-named. There is no transitional read path; the parser
      does not auto-detect the old schemas, does not auto-rewrite, does
      not silently default any missing section to an empty string, does
      not silently translate a retired marker name to its renamed
      successor, and there is no `aide_upgrade` carve-out that
      auto-injects the missing pairs or rewrites the retired names.
      Users hand-edit their file: rename their existing markers to the
      new names and paste in the new `config` and `update-playbook`
      sections."
    - >-
      "Failure to read or parse the file collapses to a structured tagged
      result — never throws. Explicit failure shapes: file missing (no
      `.aide/config/brain.aide` at the expected path), frontmatter
      malformed (YAML parse failure, required field missing among `name`
      / `mcpServerConfig.command` / `mcpServerConfig.args`, or any
      removed-and-now-rejected field present — `connector`, `rootPath`,
      `entryFile`, `tools`), body malformed (any of the six marker
      layout violations named in the marker-layout outcome above,
      including missing pairs among the six required pairs, malformed
      marker tokens, unmatched openers or closers, wrong section order,
      and nested markers)."
    - >-
      "Rejects deprecated fields explicitly. If parsed frontmatter
      contains any of `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, or `tools`,
      the parser returns `malformed-frontmatter` with a reason that names
      every stale field it found. The parser does NOT silently strip and
      ignore them; rejection forces the user to migrate cleanly to the
      two-field schema rather than carrying dead fields forward into a
      file the package has stopped honoring."
    - >-
      "Provides an interpolation helper applied at install time only:
      given the parsed frontmatter, walks `mcpServerConfig.args`
      element-by-element and returns a copy where every string element's
      `${fieldName}` placeholders have been substituted with the
      frontmatter's matching field value (today the only resolvable name
      is `${name}`; the surface generalizes to any future top-level
      frontmatter key). Null entries pass through unchanged — they are
      not strings, so there is nothing to substitute, and the helper
      preserves them verbatim so the unwired-slot signal survives the
      install-time substitution pass intact. This is the ONLY
      substitution surface in the package; it runs only for
      `.mcp.json`-writer consumers (the install service and sync verb)
      and never runs against any of the six body sections. The default
      scaffold contains no `${...}` placeholders in args (a slot the
      user has not yet wired carries a YAML null entry the config flow
      later replaces with a real string), so the helper is a no-op for
      default installs and exists for advanced users who want to DRY a
      value across positions."
    - >-
      "The parser never branches on `name`. The `name` field is treated
      as descriptive metadata only — the user-supplied label that
      surfaces in `aide_info` and in `brain.aide` itself, never a
      dispatcher. Different brains may share names without affecting
      code behavior. The fixed `brain` MCP key is the only routing
      surface this module knows about."
    - >-
      "Pure detection. The parser reads the file and parses it; it never
      writes, never spawns, never networks, never stats a path embedded
      in args, never introspects args to infer connector identity. Every
      consumer's mutation logic lives in the consumer (sync writes
      `.mcp.json`, provisionBrain scaffolds the file and the on-disk
      artifacts)."
    - >-
      "Storage-agnostic vocabulary across this spec and the parser's
      code. The forbidden-token list — `vault`, `obsidian`, `note` (used
      as a synonym for \"file\"), `wikilink`, `hub note`, and `hub` —
      does not appear in this spec's body text, outcomes, or references,
      nor in the parser module's identifiers, comments, or string
      constants.
      The six body sections are described in storage-agnostic terms
      (`orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
      `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex` — the bytes the agent reads
      live, the bytes the agent executes for backend-specific wiring,
      the bytes scaffolded as the coding-playbook entry-point artifact,
      the bytes scaffolded as the study-playbook methodology artifact,
      the bytes scaffolded as the update-playbook methodology artifact,
      and the bytes scaffolded as the research entry-point artifact).
      The parent intent's user-bytes carve-out applies to the BYTES of
      the `config`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and any other
      user-owned section content: those bytes are user-owned and may
      legitimately contain backend-specific vocabulary (the bundled
      obsidian-flavored default ships obsidian patterns inside `config`,
      `studyPlaybook`, and `updatePlaybook`), but the parser code and
      field names that handle those sections stay storage-agnostic —
      the fields are `config`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, never
      `obsidianConfig`, `studyPlaybookHub`, or anything backend-flavored.
      The anti-example carve-out applies: naming a forbidden token
      inside an undesired-outcome block to forbid it is the mechanism,
      not a violation."
  undesired:
    - >-
      "Returning the body as a single freeform string and forcing
      consumers to extract the orientation, config, playbook-index,
      study-playbook, update-playbook, or research-index section
      themselves. The parser owns body section extraction; if any
      consumer has to slice marker pairs out of a `body` field, the
      parser has decentralized the format it exists to centralize and
      every consumer becomes a second copy of the body grammar."
    - >-
      "Heading-based body parsing of any shape — looking for
      `## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub` /
      `## Orientation` / `## Config` lines, treating `^## .+$` as a
      section boundary, or accepting either marker-based OR
      heading-based input as a transitional read path. The body schema
      is exactly the paired HTML-comment markers; a heading-only file
      returns `malformed-body`."
    - >-
      "Tolerating marker tokens that vary from the lowercase
      case-sensitive form — uppercase variants like
      `<!-- AIDE-ORIENTATION-START -->`, mixed-case like
      `<!-- Aide-orientation-start -->`, stray whitespace inside the
      comment delimiters, or alternative shapes like
      `<!--aide-orientation-start-->` without surrounding spaces. The
      parser matches the literal lowercase tokens with the precise
      shape `<!-- <token>-start -->` / `<!-- <token>-end -->` exactly,
      for every one of the six section tokens (`aide-orientation`,
      `aide-config`, `aide-playbook-index`, `aide-study-playbook`,
      `aide-update-playbook`, `aide-research-index`)."
    - >-
      "Tolerating any marker layout violation as a recoverable parse —
      missing required pair (any of the six), malformed or typo'd
      marker, unmatched closer without prior opener, opener without
      closer, sections in the wrong order (config before orientation,
      playbook-index before config, study-playbook before
      playbook-index, update-playbook before study-playbook,
      research-index before update-playbook), or nested markers (an
      orientation pair containing a config pair, a config pair
      containing a playbook-index pair, a playbook-index pair containing
      a study-playbook pair, etc.). Each is `malformed-body` with a
      reason naming the violating marker; the parser does not patch,
      reorder, or guess at user intent."
    - >-
      "Treating bytes outside the marker pairs as a parse error. Notes-
      to-self above the first opener, scratch text between any closer
      and the next opener, comments after the last closer must not
      surface as `malformed-body` — those regions are not the parser's
      concern, and policing them would force users into a no-comments
      regime the schema does not require."
    - >-
      "Branching on `name` to vary parse behavior, validation rules, or
      default-value insertion. `name` is the user's label; if the parser
      dispatches on it, the package re-introduces the identity-inference
      anti-pattern the connector-registry retirement was meant to
      delete. Different brains may share `name` values; nothing in the
      parser may treat that as meaningful."
    - >-
      "Accepting deprecated fields by silently stripping or ignoring
      them. `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, and `tools` are
      removed from the schema; their presence in a parsed file is a
      structured failure, not a tolerated wart. Silent acceptance lets
      users carry the old shape forward indefinitely while believing
      their file is honored, then surfaces as drift when they wonder
      why their `tools.read` declaration has no effect."
    - >-
      "Re-introducing `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools.*`, or any other
      schema field whose only consumer is the parser's own validation.
      The schema's minimum justifies its maximum: every frontmatter
      field must name a real runtime consumer (sync writes
      `mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as a label).
      Adding a frontmatter field with no code path that reads its
      value is validation theater and is the exact regression the
      schema collapse was meant to prevent."
    - >-
      "Introspecting `mcpServerConfig.args` to extract a brain-root
      path, infer connector identity, derive a `rootPath`, or stat any
      derived path. Path validation is the launcher's job at MCP
      server startup; the package does not second-guess it. Reading
      args for any purpose other than passing them through to the
      `.mcp.json` writer is the same regression as a `rootPath` field
      in different clothing."
    - >-
      "Coercing null entries in `mcpServerConfig.args` to empty string,
      dropping them from the array, substituting any string sentinel in
      their place, compacting the array to remove them, or otherwise
      transforming the structural unwired-slot signal at parse time. Null
      is part of the args contract; the parser preserves null entries
      verbatim and at their original index positions. Coercion at the
      parse layer would silently break sync's null-refusal,
      buildBrainState's null-detection, and the warning surface
      consumers depend on to tell users their slot is unwired."
    - >-
      "Branching on string-shaped placeholders in args during parsing —
      recognizing `<BRAIN_PATH>` or any other angle-bracketed string,
      magic UUID, or `__UNWIRED__`-shape token as a special parse-level
      signal that gates validation, triggers a warning, or steers
      downstream behavior. The parser sees only YAML structure;
      sentinel-aware logic does not exist at any layer. The unwired-slot
      signal is YAML null, not a literal string."
    - >-
      "Rejecting null entries in `mcpServerConfig.args` as
      `malformed-frontmatter`. Null is part of the args type contract,
      not a malformed value — a YAML file whose `args` array carries
      null at one or more indexes is well-formed and parses to a
      well-formed `BrainAideConfig`. Treating null as malformed at the
      parse layer would force users to scaffold an unwired brain.aide
      with no path-shaped slot, breaking the contract that the parser
      is the single canonical reader and that nulls flow through to
      consumers untouched."
    - >-
      "Applying interpolation to any of the body sections.
      `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
      `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` all return verbatim; mixing
      the install-time substitution surface with the body-section
      surfaces forces every author to think about which `${...}` strings
      will be rewritten in which section, exactly the failure mode the
      parent spec's bad example calls out. Substitution lives in
      `mcpServerConfig.args` only."
    - >-
      "Throwing on missing or malformed input. Failure modes are
      structured (tagged result variants) so consumers can branch and
      compose remediation; an MCP-protocol error from a thrown parser
      gives the agent no actionable surface."
    - >-
      "Server-side defaults injected when frontmatter fields or required
      body sections are absent. The user owns the file; if a required
      field is missing, the parser surfaces it as a
      malformed-frontmatter result, and if a required marker pair is
      missing (any of the six), the parser surfaces it as a
      malformed-body result. The consumer composes a remediation
      message. The package never silently fills in `name: obsidian`, a
      default `mcpServerConfig.command` of `npx`, or default
      orientation / config / playbook-index / study-playbook /
      update-playbook / research-index bytes."
    - >-
      "A second copy of the file format encoded in any consumer.
      `buildBrainState`, the brain tool, the install service, and sync
      all derive from this parser. Re-reading the YAML or
      re-extracting any body section in any of them re-decentralizes
      the format the parser exists to centralize."
    - >-
      "A consumer reading a body section it does not own. The runtime
      brain tool reads `orientation` and `config` only — never
      `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, or
      `researchIndex`. The brain provisioner reads `playbookIndex`,
      `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` — never
      `orientation` or `config`. The `study-playbook` and
      `update-playbook` skills, and any agent following the playbook or
      research indexes, reach their content through the on-disk
      artifacts the provisioner scaffolds (`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
      `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
      `research/research.md`), not through the brain.aide
      `studyPlaybook` / `updatePlaybook` / `playbookIndex` /
      `researchIndex` body sections directly — no runtime tool surface
      reads any of those four seed sections from this parser.
      Cross-section reads couple consumers to fields they have no
      contract over and reintroduce the every-consumer-knows-the-whole-file
      regression the named-section shape exists to prevent."
    - >-
      "Module-private types for the parsed shape. The `BrainAideConfig`
      type — exactly `{ name: string; mcpServerConfig: { command:
      string; args: string[] } }` — and the parser's tagged-result
      variants (carrying `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
      `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` on the
      `ok` variant) live in `src/types/index.ts` so consumers reference
      shared definitions. A consumer that has to
      `import type { ParsedBrainAide } from '@/service/parseBrainAide'`
      reaches into module internals, breaking the central-types
      invariant."
    - >-
      "Forbidden-token leakage in this spec or in the parser's code.
      The tokens `vault`, `obsidian`, `note` (used as a synonym for
      \"file\"), `wikilink`, `hub note`, and `hub` must not appear in
      this spec's body text, outcomes, or references, nor in the parser
      module's identifiers, comments, or string constants. Field names
      like `playbookIndexHub`, `studyPlaybookHub`, `updatePlaybookHub`,
      `researchIndexHub`, `orientationHub`, or `configHub` (carrying
      the retired `hub` suffix in the typed result) are the same
      regression in different clothing — the storage-agnostic field
      names are `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
      `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex`, and they
      match the marker token base names verbatim (kebab-case in
      markers, camelCase in the typed result). Equally forbidden: the
      retired field names `prose`, `playbook`, and `research` appearing
      anywhere in the typed result or the parser code as if they were
      still part of the schema — those names belong to a prior
      vocabulary the rework retired and are now `orientation`,
      `playbookIndex`, and `researchIndex` respectively. Naming a
      forbidden token inside an undesired-outcome block to forbid it
      (this bullet, plus the migration outcome that names the retired
      `aide-prose-*` / `aide-playbook-*` / `aide-research-*` markers)
      is the carve-out, not a violation."
---

## Context

Before this module exists, the package's brain layer treats the host's
`.mcp.json` `brain` entry as the authoritative source for what brain is
wired and where its data lives. After the refactor settled in the parent
intent spec, the source of truth is a hand-written file the host owns —
`.aide/config/brain.aide`, sitting under the new user-owned
`.aide/config/` scaffold-once-never-overwritten directory — and
`.mcp.json` becomes a derived artifact the host's `npx aidemd-mcp sync`
writes from that file. Four consumers all need to read the same
brain.aide and agree on what they see: the boot reporter detecting
drift, the runtime brain tool returning agent-facing prose verbatim,
the install service scaffolding fresh installs and seeding the brain's
entry-point artifacts, and the sync verb writing the derived `.mcp.json`
entry. Without a single parser, each consumer would re-implement YAML
reading, body section extraction, and frontmatter validation — the
exact decentralization the brain backend registry's retirement was
meant to eliminate.

The parser is therefore peer-level service code, not a helper of any
single consumer. It sits at `src/service/parseBrainAide/` alongside
`install/`, `buildBrainState/`, and (post-retirement) the deleted
`brainBackends/` slot. Two consumers in `src/service/`, one in
`src/tools/`, and one in `src/cli/` all depend on it; placing it under
any single consumer would force the others to reach across a sibling
boundary the playbook's horizontal-isolation rule forbids.

The body has grown across four schema generations. The first was
heading-based — body sections were marked by Markdown headings the
parser scanned for (the retired heading names are enumerated in the
frontmatter's strict-failure-migration outcome where naming them is
the mechanism that forbids them). The second was the marker-pair
pivot to three named sections (`prose`, `playbook`, `research`)
delimited by paired HTML-comment markers, which landed the playbook
and research entry-point artifacts. The third extended that to four
named sections by inserting a `studyPlaybook` section between
`playbook` and `research`, splitting playbook-navigation methodology
out of the playbook-conventions section so the agent-side
`study-playbook` skill could read its navigation prose from an
on-disk artifact rather than from a package-shipped skill prompt.
The fourth — the schema this rework lands — extends the body to six
named sections by inserting a `config` section between `orientation`
(the renamed `prose` section) and `playbookIndex` (the renamed
`playbook` section), and an `updatePlaybook` section between
`studyPlaybook` and `researchIndex` (the renamed `research` section).
The renames are deliberate: section names should describe what the
section IS, not just what's in them. `orientation` describes the
agent's runtime briefing better than `prose`; `playbookIndex` and
`researchIndex` make clear they seed the *index* of their entry-point
tree, not the underlying content the index points at; `studyPlaybook`
and `updatePlaybook` carry no suffix because they seed methodology
files, not indexes.

The two new sections embody a structural axis the prior schemas did
not have: the live-vs-seed split. The first two body sections
(`orientation`, `config`) are agent-facing live sections — the
runtime brain tool reads them on demand whenever an agent calls
`aide_brain`, and they live in `brain.aide` permanently. Changes to
them happen only when the user edits `brain.aide`. The remaining
four sections (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
`researchIndex`) are install-time seeds — the install service reads
them once on cold install and writes their bytes into the brain as
on-disk files; after install the brain owns those files and the user
edits them in the brain, while the original brain.aide section bytes
go dormant (they only matter again on a fresh re-init into an empty
brain root). This split is why the schema needed to extend rather
than fork into multiple files: every section is content the user
owns, every section flows through the same paired-marker grammar,
and the parser's single-file-multiple-consumers chokepoint shape
already handles the partition cleanly — the same parser hands the
runtime tool its live pair and the install service its seed quartet,
each consumer touching only its own typed keys.

The `config` section — new in this rework — exists because the
shipped `/aide:brain config` command was carrying integration-specific
orchestration that belongs at the host layer. Its argument vocabulary
varies by backend (one brain wants an absolute path; another wants
an API token plus a page id; a third wants a JSON blob), and any
package-side dispatch on backend identity is the connector-registry
anti-pattern the parent spec retired. Moving the wiring flow into a
user-owned body section that the runtime tool returns verbatim turns
the shipped command into a thin router that forwards `$ARGUMENTS`
opaquely; the integration's own prose interprets the bytes. The
section lives in `brain.aide` permanently (not as an install seed)
because it's read before the brain is wired, on every config-flow
invocation.

The `updatePlaybook` section — also new in this rework — seeds a
playbook-maintenance methodology artifact at
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, sibling to the
study-playbook artifact. It's a seed (not a live `aide_brain` kind)
because after install, the live source of truth is the on-disk
artifact in the brain, where the user edits it directly to evolve
their maintenance methodology — same lifecycle as `studyPlaybook`.
The shipped `/aide:update-playbook` command becomes a one-paragraph
pointer at the on-disk artifact, mirroring the `study-playbook`
skill pattern.

The frontmatter schema is the parent spec's load-bearing minimum:
exactly `name` (a label) and `mcpServerConfig` (consumed by sync).
Every other field the prior schema carried — `connector`, `rootPath`,
`entryFile`, `tools` — has been removed because no runtime code
consumed it; their presence in a parsed file was validation theater.
The parser's job is to enforce that minimum: accept the two-field
shape, reject deprecated fields by name so users migrate cleanly, and
return a typed structure whose surface area matches the schema's
surface area exactly. Brain location lives once, inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args`; the parser never derives a logical root from
args, never stats a path, never introspects args to infer connector
identity. The interface contract for what MCP tools the agent calls
lives in the `orientation` body section (the bytes between
`<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` and `<!-- aide-orientation-end -->`),
not in frontmatter — the agent reads the orientation verbatim, calls
whatever it names, and surfaces failures fast. The integration's
wiring flow lives in the `config` body section (the bytes between
`<!-- aide-config-start -->` and `<!-- aide-config-end -->`), read
on demand by the runtime tool when the config flow is invoked.

The unwired-slot signal is the second load-bearing decision and is
where this rework changes the contract directly. `mcpServerConfig.args`
is typed `(string | null)[]` — every element is either a fully-formed
argument string OR YAML null (a bare `-` with no scalar after, which
YAML 1.1/1.2 parses to JS `null`). YAML null at any index is the
explicit unwired-slot signal: the bytes the user has not yet wired,
the position the `/aide:brain config` flow asks the user about and
edits in place. The parser preserves null entries verbatim — same
type, same index — and three downstream consumers depend on that
preservation to recognize the unwired state structurally: the sync
verb's null-refusal precondition, `buildBrainState`'s null-detection
precedence rule that drives the unwired-slot warning surface, and
the install-time `interpolateArgs` helper's null-passthrough. If the
parser coerced null to empty string, dropped null entries, or
rejected nulls as malformed-frontmatter, every one of those
consumers would see a string where the YAML carried structure and
would lose the ability to distinguish "wired with empty bytes" from
"unwired by user intent" — the exact byte-for-byte-agreement bug the
prior literal-sentinel design produced when a `<BRAIN_PATH>`
placeholder string round-tripped through drift checks unchanged.

The interpolation surface is the third load-bearing decision but is
nearly dormant by default. The parent spec partitions substitution
into two surfaces with different audiences: install-time interpolation
of `mcpServerConfig.args` (the `.mcp.json` writer's audience) and
agent-or-disk-time body sections (the agent and the install
scaffolder's audiences). The same parser knows about both surfaces
because both derive from the same parsed file, but the helper that
performs interpolation is exposed separately so consumers that don't
need it (the brain tool returning live sections, the brain
provisioner scaffolding entry-point artifacts, the boot reporter
checking drift on disk-shape) cannot accidentally invoke it. With
the schema collapsed to `name` + `mcpServerConfig`, the only
frontmatter value resolvable by `${...}` is `${name}`, and the
default scaffold contains no `${...}` placeholders at all — the
helper exists for advanced users who want to DRY a value across
positions. The interpolation surface composes cleanly with the
null-as-unwired contract: `interpolateArgs` walks args
element-by-element, substitutes `${name}`-style placeholders inside
string elements, and leaves null entries entirely alone (there is
no string to substitute into). Substitution runs only on
`mcpServerConfig.args` string elements and never on any of the six
body sections, keeping the parent spec's bad example (server-side
substitution leaking into any body section) impossible by
construction.

## Strategy

**Single canonical reader, four consumers.** The boot reporter, the
runtime brain tool, the install scaffolder, and the sync verb all
import from this module. None of them re-parses YAML, none of them
extracts a body section independently, none of them validates required
frontmatter fields. Centralizing format knowledge here means a future
change to the brain.aide schema — a new marker pair, a new required
frontmatter field, a tightening of the body grammar — is a one-file
edit; the consumers see the new shape automatically. This is the same
chokepoint pattern the install service's `initContent` already uses
for canonical docs — one reader, many composers — and it is what
makes the live-vs-seed split enforceable: each consumer imports only
the typed keys it owns, and a consumer reaching for a key it has no
contract over fails type-check before it fails runtime.

**Six named body sections, extracted in one parse pass; marker pairs
not headings.** The body grammar is a small enumerated set of paired
HTML-comment markers — six pairs, twelve markers total, each section
opened and closed by its own distinct token — recognized lowercase
and case-sensitive in the precise shape `<!-- <token>-start -->` /
`<!-- <token>-end -->`. The required order is `orientation` →
`config` → `playbook-index` → `study-playbook` → `update-playbook` →
`research-index`. The choice of paired HTML-comment markers over
Markdown headings is load-bearing: heading-based parsing forces the
parser to treat any `^## .+$` line as a potential section boundary,
which means every word a user types in a heading position becomes
schema, and every casual heading in their own prose risks tripping
the grammar. Marker pairs are tokens the parser owns and the user
quotes verbatim — a closed grammar with twelve recognized strings
and silence outside them. The convention extends the existing
`writeMethodology` HTML-comment marker precedent (same lowercase
shape, distinct open/close per section); the parser walks the body
once after frontmatter validation, locates each marker pair, and
slices the bytes between each opener and its matching closer. The
parsed result on the `ok` branch carries each section as its own
typed field — `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex` — kebab-case in
the markers, camelCase in the typed keys, the same word in both
surfaces.

**Fixed section order, not free-order.** The six pairs must appear
in the canonical order, top-to-bottom. Free-order ("any of the six
pairs in any order") would force the parser to scan the entire body
for each marker independently and would let users encode containment
hierarchies (research-index above orientation, config inside the
playbook-index region) the schema does not have. Fixed order makes
the parse a single forward walk that fails at the first out-of-order
opener, lets the bundled scaffold's section layout match the wild
files the parser will see, and turns "which section comes first" out
of a per-host stylistic choice into a guaranteed property every
consumer can rely on without inspecting. The order also encodes the
live-vs-seed axis structurally: the two live sections sit first, the
four seed sections sit second, grouped by destination directory
(`coding-playbook/*` then `research/*`).

**Per-consumer ownership is exclusive across the live-vs-seed
split.** The runtime brain tool reads `orientation` and `config` —
never `playbookIndex`, never `studyPlaybook`, never `updatePlaybook`,
never `researchIndex`. The install service's brain provisioner reads
`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and
`researchIndex` to scaffold the four entry-point artifacts on disk —
never `orientation`, never `config`. The sync verb and the boot
reporter read frontmatter only — none of the body sections. This
exclusive ownership is the structural property that makes the
live-vs-seed split real rather than naming-only: if `aide_brain` ever
returned a seed section's bytes, the agent would have two
authoritative sources for the same content (the brain.aide section
AND the on-disk artifact in the brain), and the agent's edits would
target the wrong one. If the install service ever read `orientation`
or `config`, it would be reaching for content the user expects to
live in `brain.aide` permanently and treating it as install-time
seed material — a category mistake the install service is forbidden
from making. The named-section shape makes those ownership lines
visible in the type signatures every consumer imports; a consumer
reaching outside its slice imports a key its module's spec does not
authorize.

**`update-playbook` is a seed, never a live `aide_brain` kind.** The
runtime brain tool's `kind` parameter accepts exactly two values —
`"orientation"` (the default) and `"config"` — corresponding to the
two live sections. `update-playbook` is structurally a seed: after
install, its authoritative bytes live at
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` in the brain itself, where the
user edits them to evolve the maintenance methodology. Exposing
`kind: "update-playbook"` would create exactly the dual-source
problem the live-vs-seed split exists to prevent — agents would
have two locations to read from (the brain.aide section AND the
on-disk artifact) and no clear contract about which to edit. The
shipped `/aide:update-playbook` command therefore mirrors the
existing `study-playbook` skill pattern: it's a one-paragraph pointer
that calls `aide_brain` for the brain's read tool, reads the
on-disk artifact, and follows the methodology that artifact
describes. The same argument applies to `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, and `researchIndex` — none of them are reachable
through `aide_brain` because their authoritative post-install bytes
live on disk, not in `brain.aide`.

**Strict marker layout; violations are malformed-body naming the
violator.** Any marker layout violation — a required pair missing
(any of the six), a marker token malformed or typo'd, a closing
marker without a prior matching opener, an opener without a closer,
the six pairs appearing out of the required order, or any nesting of
markers inside another marker pair — returns `malformed-body` with a
reason naming the violating marker. The parser does NOT case-fold
the tokens, does NOT tolerate uppercase or mixed-case variants, does
NOT accept stray whitespace inside the comment delimiters, and does
NOT attempt recovery. Bytes outside any marker pair are silently
ignored: the parser's job is to extract the six named sections, not
to police the rest of the file. The grammar is closed and
reviewable: every recognized marker pair has a typed field on the
result and a downstream consumer that owns it; every layout
violation is a structured failure the user resolves by editing.

**Verbatim invariant generalized to every body section.** The parser
returns each body section byte-identical to what the user wrote
between its opening marker and its matching closing marker. No
trimming, no normalization, no markdown rewriting, no template
engine. This was previously a guarantee about the prose section
only; with the body grown to six sections the same invariant covers
all six. A user who hand-edits their `config` section to teach a
novel argument shape (a JSON blob, a token-plus-id pair, a
multi-arg flag list) gets exactly those bytes returned by
`aide_brain` when the config flow runs; a user who hand-edits their
`updatePlaybook` section to teach a different drift-check
methodology gets exactly those bytes scaffolded as the on-disk
artifact; a user who embeds literal `${variable}` characters in
`orientation` because their orientation prose documents a templating
language gets those characters preserved. The parent spec's
no-substitution-on-body-content invariant covers every section the
parser returns, not just the historically singular prose body.

**Tagged-result return shape, never throws.** The parser returns a
discriminated union: on the `ok` variant, the typed frontmatter
fields (`name`, `mcpServerConfig`) sit alongside the six typed body
fields (`orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) as siblings on the result —
exactly what the parent spec mandates. The other variants are
`{ kind: "missing" }`, `{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason }`,
and `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason }`. Every consumer branches on
`kind` and composes its own remediation prose. The body field named
`config` (the integration's wiring-flow prose) sits as a peer of the
frontmatter fields rather than nested under any `config` wrapper —
the prior shape that wrapped frontmatter under `config` is retired
because the body now has a section literally named `config` and a
nested `result.config.config` access path is the kind of disambig
churn the central-types invariant exists to prevent. Throwing would
surface as an MCP protocol error with no `instructions` field for
the agent to act on, defeating the parent spec's "structured-state
at every layer" invariant.

**Strict-failure migration policy: every pre-rework shape surfaces
as malformed-body naming every expected new marker.** Three
pre-rework shapes exist in the wild and all three fail strictly.
First: a heading-based `brain.aide` (the original schema, body
marked by `## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub`
headings with no marker pairs anywhere) returns `malformed-body`
naming all twelve expected new markers. Second: a three-pair
marker-bounded `brain.aide` (the post-pivot pre-amendment schema,
with `aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`, and `aide-research-*` pairs
only) returns `malformed-body` naming every expected new marker.
Third — the case this rework specifically introduces — a four-pair
marker-bounded `brain.aide` (the post-amendment pre-rework schema,
with `aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`, `aide-study-playbook-*`, and
`aide-research-*` pairs) returns `malformed-body` naming every
expected new marker that is missing or retired-named: the missing
`<!-- aide-config-start -->` / `-end` pair, the missing
`<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->` / `-end` pair, and the four
retired-named pairs that the user must rename to
`<!-- aide-orientation-* -->`, `<!-- aide-playbook-index-* -->`, and
`<!-- aide-research-index-* -->` (the `<!-- aide-study-playbook-* -->`
pair keeps its name across the rework). In none of the three cases
does the parser auto-detect the old shape, auto-rewrite, silently
default the missing sections to empty strings, silently translate a
retired marker name to its renamed successor, or carve out an
`aide_upgrade`-time exception that auto-injects the missing pairs
or rewrites the retired names. The user hand-edits their file. This
is the same strict-failure policy the prior 3→4 amendment used; it
holds across the 4→6 rework for the same reason. An auto-rename or
auto-injection path would land package-authored bytes inside the
user-owned file — exactly the package-as-vote anti-pattern the
brain-config user-ownership invariant was designed to prevent —
because the new sections (`config`, `updatePlaybook`) carry
integration-specific orchestration that the package cannot author
for the user without re-introducing connector-side dispatch.

**YAML null is the unwired-slot signal; the parser preserves it
verbatim and never introspects literal-string sentinels.** The
`mcpServerConfig.args` contract is `(string | null)[]`. When the
install service scaffolds `brain.aide` for an integration whose
launcher needs a value the user has not yet supplied (the
cold-start case where the user has not yet told the package where
their brain root lives, or has not yet supplied an API token, or
has not yet provided whatever per-integration value the slot
represents), the bundled default template lands a YAML null at that
index — a bare `-` with no scalar after — and the parser preserves
that null verbatim. Index positions in the parsed args match index
positions in the source YAML one-to-one: no compaction, no
reordering, no coercion to empty string, no substitution of any
placeholder string. The unwired-slot signal is structural — it
lives in the YAML type system, which is also TypeScript's type
system at the parser boundary — not lexical, which is what closes
the byte-for-byte-agreement bug class that an earlier
literal-string sentinel design produced. A literal string like
`<BRAIN_PATH>` (the retired anti-pattern this rework replaces)
round-trips through drift checks unchanged because string equality
on `<BRAIN_PATH>` looks identical to string equality on a real
path with the same characters; YAML null is structurally distinct
from every string at the YAML, JS, and TypeScript layers, so every
consumer that needs to ask "is this slot wired?" gets a real
answer from the parser's typed result without pattern-matching
strings. The parser does NOT recognize `<BRAIN_PATH>`,
`<API_TOKEN>`, `__UNWIRED__`, magic UUIDs, or any other
angle-bracketed or otherwise sentinel-shaped string as a
parse-level signal — every string in args is just a string the
parser passes through verbatim. Sentinel-aware logic does not
exist at any layer of the package; that knowledge would couple
the parser to one integration's placeholder convention and force
every future placeholder shape to round-trip through this module.

**Null passthrough is mandatory and tested at every transformation
the parser exposes.** Three downstream consumers read nulls
structurally and depend on the parser preserving them: the sync
verb's null-refusal precondition halts when any null is present in
the args it would write; `buildBrainState`'s null-detection rule
takes precedence over args-disagreement checks so an unwired slot
surfaces as "unwired" rather than "disagreement"; and
`interpolateArgs` walks args element-by-element and skips null
entries entirely (there is no string to substitute into). Each of
those contracts assumes the parser preserves the YAML null
faithfully — typed as `null`, at the original index, with no
neighbor compaction. Coercion at the parse layer would silently
break all three: sync would write a `.mcp.json` entry whose empty
string is indistinguishable from a wired-but-empty string,
buildBrainState would lose the precedence signal that drives the
warning surface, and `interpolateArgs` would have nothing to
preserve through. The verbatim invariant is therefore not just
about strings — every element of args is preserved as the type
YAML produced (string or null), at the index YAML produced it.

**Substitution is exposed as a separate helper, not folded into the
parse step, and passes nulls through unchanged.** The parser returns
frontmatter and every body section verbatim. A second exported
function — `interpolateArgs(config) → (string | null)[]` — takes the
parsed config and applies frontmatter-value substitution to
`mcpServerConfig.args` element-by-element: string elements get their
`${...}` placeholders resolved against top-level frontmatter
fields; null elements pass through unchanged because there is no
string to substitute into. The return type is the same
`(string | null)[]` shape the parser produces, with the same
indexes — interpolation is a per-string transform, not an
array-shape transform. Two consumers call this helper (the install
service's brain provisioner deriving the MCP entry, the sync verb
writing `.mcp.json`); the rest do not (the runtime brain tool
returning live sections, the brain provisioner scaffolding seed
artifacts, the boot reporter checking drift). Splitting interpolation
into an opt-in helper means a consumer that never imports
`interpolateArgs` cannot accidentally apply substitution to something
it shouldn't — none of the six body sections has any path through
the exported surface that runs interpolation on it. Equally
important: keeping nulls intact through `interpolateArgs` means the
unwired-slot signal survives the install-time substitution pass
intact, so sync's null-refusal sees a null at the same index even
after the parser has handed off to the helper.

**Interpolation is generalized over frontmatter fields, not
field-specific.** The substitution engine walks each string in `args`
looking for `${<key>}` patterns and replaces them with the matching
top-level frontmatter value. With the schema collapsed to `name` +
`mcpServerConfig`, the only resolvable placeholder today is
`${name}`; the default scaffold uses no placeholders at all and the
helper is a no-op for default installs. The engine generalizes
naturally if the schema ever grows — no field-specific privilege.
Crucially, the engine does NOT know about environment variables,
shell metacharacters, or any other expansion source — substitution
is purely frontmatter-value-to-args-position.

**`name` is metadata, never dispatched on.** The parser reads `name`
from frontmatter and includes it in the returned config (consumers
may surface it in `aide_info` for human-readable display). But no
parser code branches on its value, and different brains may share
names without affecting code behavior. If a future consumer wants
per-brain behavior, the right move is to push that decision back
into the user's `brain.aide` (where the user authors brain-specific
orientation and config bytes verbatim, and brain-specific seed bytes
for every entry-point artifact), not to add a registry to this
module. The parent spec's no-connector-branching invariant makes
this load-bearing under every renamed field.

**Schema is exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` with
`args` typed `(string | null)[]`; deprecated fields are rejected, not
stripped.** Required frontmatter fields are `name`,
`mcpServerConfig.command`, and `mcpServerConfig.args`; absence of any
of these returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: <which field> }`. The
`args` array's element type is `string | null` — null entries are
part of the contract, not malformed values, and the parser does NOT
return `malformed-frontmatter` for an args array that carries one or
more nulls. A YAML file whose `args` is a mix of strings and bare-`-`
nulls is well-formed and parses to a well-formed config. Presence
of any removed field — `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
`tools` — returns `malformed-frontmatter` with a reason that names
every stale field it found. The parser does NOT silently strip
deprecated fields; rejection forces clean migration rather than
indefinite drift. Defaults are never injected for missing required
fields, and null entries in args are never replaced with package-side
default values either — the user's edit (including null-as-unwired)
is authoritative; package-side defaults would re-introduce the
"vote the package gets to override" pattern the parent spec's bad
examples explicitly rule out.

**Retired vocabulary stays retired.** The retired field names —
`prose`, `playbook`, `research` — must not appear anywhere in the
parser's typed result, the parser's code, identifier names, comment
strings, or test fixtures as if they were still part of the schema.
The current vocabulary is `orientation`, `playbookIndex`,
`researchIndex` (renamed from `prose`, `playbook`, `research`
respectively); `studyPlaybook` retains its name from the prior
amendment; `config` and `updatePlaybook` are new. Equally retired:
the `Hub`-suffix shape on every seed-section field
(`playbookIndexHub`, `studyPlaybookHub`, `updatePlaybookHub`,
`researchIndexHub`) — that suffix is the regression class that was
rejected at the first three-section pivot and stays rejected through
every subsequent extension. The parser's typed keys are exactly the
six camelCase names above and nothing else. The retired marker
tokens (`aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`, `aide-research-*`) appear
in this spec only inside the strict-failure-migration outcome where
naming them is the mechanism that forbids them, never as live
recognized markers in the parser code.

**Types live centrally in `src/types/`, not module-private; `args` is
typed `(string | null)[]` at the central type definition.** The
`BrainAideConfig` shape — `{ name: string; mcpServerConfig: { command:
string; args: (string | null)[] } }` — the `BrainAideMcpServerConfig`
slice, and the `ParseBrainAideResult` tagged union (including the six
body section fields `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex` on the `ok`
variant) all live in `src/types/index.ts` and are imported by every
consumer. The parser does not export its own type definitions for
the parsed shape. The `(string | null)[]` typing of `args` is the
load-bearing surface that lets every downstream consumer branch on
null structurally rather than pattern-matching strings: sync's
null-refusal types its precondition against the central type;
`buildBrainState`'s null-detection types its precedence rule
against the central type; `interpolateArgs` types its element-walk
against the central type. A schema change is one edit in one file,
with type errors propagating to every downstream module that
depends on the changed shape.

**Single-helper file layout.** The module has one orchestrator export
(`parseBrainAide`), one auxiliary export (`interpolateArgs`), and an
exported in-memory string parser sibling (`parseBrainAideFromString`)
that the install service uses to derive the MCP entry from template
bytes that have not been written to disk yet. All three fit
comfortably under ~200 lines combined and do not warrant separate
sub-helper folders. The progressive-disclosure "single-helper
exception" applies — they stay in one `index.ts`, with helpers
defined above the default export to honor the function-ordering
rule. When the file format grows (a new tagged-result variant, a
new marker pair, environment-variable-style expansions), factor each
responsibility into its own subfolder.

**Pure detection, no I/O orchestration of `.mcp.json`.** The parser
reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` and parses it. It does not stat any
path embedded in args (path validation is the launcher's job at MCP
server startup), it does not write `.mcp.json` (that's `sync`'s job),
it does not write entry-point artifacts to disk (that's the install
service's apply layer's job), it does not enumerate the file system.
One async hop (`readFile`) on the happy path. This keeps the module's
test surface tiny and makes the consumers' tests independent — the
brain tool's tests don't have to mock filesystem walks the parser
doesn't perform.

**Storage-agnostic vocabulary across this spec and the parser's
code.** The parser describes its six body sections as `orientation`,
`config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and
`researchIndex` — the bytes the agent reads as the runtime briefing,
the bytes the agent executes for backend-specific wiring, the bytes
scaffolded as the four entry-point artifacts on cold install. The
forbidden-token list inherited from the parent spec —
connector-specific terms a single backend would use as synonyms for
"brain" or "file" or "navigation link" — does not appear in this
spec's prose, in the parser module's identifiers, in its comments,
or in its string constants. The anti-example carve-out applies one
place only: naming a forbidden token inside an undesired-outcome
block of the frontmatter to forbid it.

**Carve-out: the BYTES of the user-owned sections may legitimately
carry backend-specific vocabulary.** The parent intent's
storage-agnostic invariant covers every surface the package authors
in code. The body section bytes the bundled obsidian-flavored
default template ships are NOT a surface the package authors prose
for in the backend-agnostic sense — those bytes flow from the
bundled template, which legitimately ships obsidian-specific
patterns across the `config` section (Obsidian's
`/aide:brain config <absolute-path>` argument shape and the
edit-and-sync flow that lands an absolute path inline in args), the
`studyPlaybook` section (obsidian-specific navigation patterns), the
`updatePlaybook` section (obsidian-specific maintenance methodology
calling `mcp__brain__patch_note`), and the `playbookIndex` /
`researchIndex` sections (obsidian-flavored entry-point artifact
content). Hosts that pick a different brain edit the relevant
sections in their own `brain.aide`. The parser code, its
identifiers, its comments, its string constants, and the typed
result field names all stay storage-agnostic — the carve-out
applies to the BYTES the user controls, not to the parser that
handles them.

## Good examples

**A complete six-section `brain.aide` with a fully-wired
`mcpServerConfig.args` parses to the expected structure.** The
host's `.aide/config/brain.aide` contains:

```
---
name: my-brain
mcpServerConfig:
  command: cmd
  args:
    - /c
    - npx
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "D:/Code/Me/my-brain"
---

A note-to-self above the first opener — silently ignored by the
parser; the user can keep scratch text here without warning.

<!-- aide-orientation-start -->
Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.
Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-root-relative
path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across the
brain. The store has four entry-point artifacts: the coding-playbook
index at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, the study-playbook
methodology at `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, the
update-playbook methodology at `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`,
and the research index at `research/research.md`. Start from the
relevant entry-point for your task and follow the references it
lists.
<!-- aide-orientation-end -->

<!-- aide-config-start -->
You are completing the wiring of this brain. The required value is
the absolute path to the user's brain root, landed at `args[3]` of
`mcpServerConfig.args` in `brain.aide`. When `$ARGUMENTS` is
non-empty, treat it as the absolute path. Empty `$ARGUMENTS` against
an un-wired brain (null at `args[3]`) means ask interactively; empty
against an already-wired brain (string at `args[3]`) means STOP.
After editing `brain.aide`, sync via
`.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]`'s command plus args plus `"sync"`,
then emit the restart message.
<!-- aide-config-end -->

<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->
# Coding playbook

An entry point for coding conventions, patterns, and architecture
decisions. Read this index first, then drill into the section that
matches your task.

## Task routing

| Task type | Read first |
| --------- | ---------- |

## Sections
<!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->

<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->
# Study playbook

Read the playbook index first, then drill into the section that
matches your task. Don't re-read what you already opened in this
session.
<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->
# Update playbook

Maintenance methodology for the coding playbook. Identify the
change, locate the relevant section in the playbook index, apply
the edit, then check the routing table for drift and reconcile.
<!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-research-index-start -->
# Research

An entry point for domain research material.

## Domains

| Domain | Notes |
| ------ | ----- |
<!-- aide-research-index-end -->
```

The parser returns:

```
{
  kind: "ok",
  name: "my-brain",
  mcpServerConfig: {
    command: "cmd",
    args: ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/Code/Me/my-brain"]
  },
  orientation: "Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools...",
  config: "You are completing the wiring of this brain. The required value is...",
  playbookIndex: "# Coding playbook\n\nAn entry point for coding conventions...",
  studyPlaybook: "# Study playbook\n\nRead the playbook index first...",
  updatePlaybook: "# Update playbook\n\nMaintenance methodology for the coding playbook...",
  researchIndex: "# Research\n\nAn entry point for domain research material..."
}
```

`args` is typed `(string | null)[]`; in this fully-wired example
every element is a string. No `connector`, no `rootPath`, no
`entryFile`, no `tools` appear in the parsed shape — the schema is
exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig` in frontmatter and the six named
body fields as siblings on the result. No retired field names
(`prose`, `playbook`, `research`) appear anywhere in the typed
result. The `args` array carries the path inline byte-for-byte
under user ownership; no placeholders, no substitution. Each of the
six body section fields starts immediately after its opening marker
and ends immediately before its matching closing marker,
byte-identical to what the user wrote between those bounds. Bytes
outside any marker pair (the note-to-self above the first opener)
are silently ignored.

**A null-armed `brain.aide` (the cold-install default for an
integration whose path slot the user has not yet filled) parses
with a YAML null preserved at the unwired index.** The host's
freshly-seeded `.aide/config/brain.aide` carries a path slot the
user has not yet wired, and the bundled default lands a bare YAML
null there:

```
---
name: my-brain
mcpServerConfig:
  command: cmd
  args:
    - /c
    - npx
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    -
---
```

The fourth `args` entry is a bare `-` with no scalar after it —
YAML parses that as null. The parser returns:

```
{
  kind: "ok",
  name: "my-brain",
  mcpServerConfig: {
    command: "cmd",
    args: ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]
  },
  orientation: "...",
  config: "...",
  playbookIndex: "...",
  studyPlaybook: "...",
  updatePlaybook: "...",
  researchIndex: "..."
}
```

`args[3]` is the JS `null` value, typed `null` under
`(string | null)[]`. The array length is four — null does not
compact away. Index 3 is the null index in source YAML and is the
null index in the parsed result; downstream consumers
(`buildBrainState` detecting unwired slots, sync refusing to write
nulls, `/aide:brain config` editing the null position) all read
from the same index they would read in the wired example. No
literal-string sentinel (`<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, etc.)
appears anywhere in the parsed shape — the unwired-slot signal is
the JS null produced from the YAML null, not a placeholder string
the parser pattern-matched.

**A `brain.aide` whose body parses cleanly into all six typed
keys.** Given the wired example's body, the parser slices out
exactly six byte-ranges between the twelve markers and returns
them as the six typed body fields on the `ok` variant —
`orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`. Each field's value is the
verbatim content between its opening and closing markers; no
trimming, no normalization, no substitution. The frontmatter
fields (`name`, `mcpServerConfig`) and the body fields all sit as
siblings on the result, none nested under any wrapper, none missing
from the typed surface. Consumers branch on `kind`, then access
exactly the keys their module's spec authorizes — `aide_brain`
reads `result.orientation` and `result.config`; the brain
provisioner reads the four seed-section keys; sync and the boot
reporter read `result.mcpServerConfig` and never reach into a body
section.

**Each consumer pulls only the section it owns; live and seed
consumers never cross.** Given the parsed result above, the four
downstream consumers reach for different fields and never cross
over:

- `aide_brain` (the runtime brain tool) reads `result.orientation`
  when `kind` defaults or is `"orientation"`, and `result.config`
  when `kind === "config"`. It returns the matching section
  byte-identical as `instructions`. It NEVER touches
  `result.playbookIndex`, `result.studyPlaybook`,
  `result.updatePlaybook`, or `result.researchIndex` — those four
  seed sections do not exist as far as `aide_brain`'s contract is
  concerned, because their post-install authoritative bytes live as
  on-disk artifacts in the brain, not in `brain.aide`.
- `provisionBrain` (the install service's brain provisioner) reads
  `result.playbookIndex` to source the bytes of the coding-playbook
  entry-point InitStep, `result.studyPlaybook` for the
  study-playbook methodology InitStep, `result.updatePlaybook` for
  the update-playbook methodology InitStep, and
  `result.researchIndex` for the research entry-point InitStep. It
  NEVER touches `result.orientation` or `result.config` — those
  live sections are agent-facing runtime content, not install seed
  material. It also calls `interpolateArgs(result)` to derive the
  MCP step's args.
- `cli/sync` reads `result.mcpServerConfig` (after `interpolateArgs`
  runs against args), writes the resulting entry into `.mcp.json`
  under `mcpServers.brain`, and ignores every body section.
- The boot reporter reads `result.mcpServerConfig` (again through
  `interpolateArgs`) to compare against the actual entry in
  `.mcp.json` for drift detection. Body sections are irrelevant to
  the comparison.

The named-section shape makes those ownership lines visible in the
type signatures: a consumer reaching outside its owned slice imports
a field its module's `.aide` spec does not authorize. The
live-vs-seed split is enforced by the typed keys themselves —
`aide_brain` cannot accidentally return seed bytes because its
contract names only `orientation` and `config`; `provisionBrain`
cannot accidentally seed live bytes because its contract names only
the four seed keys.

**`aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` returns the config section
verbatim for the wiring flow.** The `/aide:brain config` shipped
command is a thin router: it forwards `$ARGUMENTS` to the agent
without parsing, calls `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })`, and
executes the returned bytes verbatim. The runtime tool reads
`result.config` from the parser and returns:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "You are completing the wiring of this brain..."
}
```

The bytes between `<!-- aide-config-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-config-end -->` come back unmodified — no template
substitution, no rendering pass, no awareness of what argument
shape the user provided. The integration's `config` prose alone
interprets the bytes; a different brain (Notion, hosted, custom)
would ship structurally different config prose under different
argument-shape conventions, and the same routing path returns it
without code change.

**Sync calls the interpolation helper on a fully-wired config; on
the default scaffold it is a no-op.** Given the wired parsed result
above:

```
interpolateArgs(result) === ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "D:/Code/Me/my-brain"]
```

The args contain no `${...}` placeholders and no nulls, so the
helper returns them unchanged. Sync writes the result into
`.mcp.json` under `mcpServers.brain` along with `command: "cmd"`.
Neither `aide_brain` (returning live sections) nor `provisionBrain`
(scaffolding seed artifacts) calls `interpolateArgs` against any of
the six body sections; they go to disk or wire verbatim.

**`interpolateArgs` against a null-armed config preserves the null
at its index.** Given the null-armed parsed result above:

```
interpolateArgs(result) === ["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]
```

The helper walks args element-by-element. The three string entries
have no `${...}` placeholders, so they pass through unchanged. The
fourth entry is `null` — there is no string to substitute into, so
the helper preserves it at its original index. The return type is
`(string | null)[]`, matching the parser's typing of
`mcpServerConfig.args`. Sync's null-refusal precondition then sees
`null` at index 3, halts, and surfaces an unwired-slot remediation
through the `/aide:brain config` flow — exactly the path the
structural-null contract is designed to drive. If the parser had
coerced the null to an empty string before `interpolateArgs` ran,
the helper would receive a string at index 3, sync's null-refusal
would never fire, and `.mcp.json` would land with an empty-string
arg the launcher would silently fail on.

**An advanced user opts into interpolation by referencing
`${name}`; null entries continue to pass through.** A user
authoring a brain whose launcher accepts the brain name as a CLI
arg writes:

```
---
name: dev-brain
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "some-launcher"
    - "--profile"
    - "${name}"
    -
---
```

(plus the six required marker-bounded body sections, unchanged
from above.) The parser returns the args verbatim, with
`"${name}"` as a literal string element and `null` as the fourth
element. `interpolateArgs(result)` substitutes the `${name}`
placeholder against the matching top-level frontmatter field, and
preserves the null at index 3, returning
`["some-launcher", "--profile", "dev-brain", null]`. The six body
sections are returned untouched even if they contain literal
`${name}` characters in their text — interpolation never reaches
any body section. Per-element interpolation composes cleanly with
per-element null passthrough; the helper's contract is per-string
substitution within the `(string | null)[]` shape, never an
array-shape transform.

**A missing `.aide/config/brain.aide` returns `{ kind: "missing" }`.**
A fresh project that has not run `aide_init` yet has no `brain.aide`
on disk. The parser tries to read it, gets ENOENT, and returns
`{ kind: "missing" }`. The boot reporter forwards this to
`aide_info.brain.status === "no-brain-aide"`. The orchestrator
HALTS and tells the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp init`.

**A frontmatter missing `name` returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: "name is required" }`.**
The user hand-edited `brain.aide` and accidentally deleted the
`name` line. The parser reads YAML, validates required fields,
detects the absence, and returns a structured failure. The same
shape applies to a missing `mcpServerConfig.command` or
`mcpServerConfig.args`.

**A frontmatter carrying deprecated fields returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: "deprecated fields:
rootPath, tools" }`.** A user upgrading from a pre-collapse
`brain.aide` runs the parser against their old file. The parser
reads YAML, sees `rootPath` and `tools`, and returns a structured
failure naming every stale field.

**A pre-rework heading-based `brain.aide` returns malformed-body
naming all twelve expected new markers.** A user whose `brain.aide`
predates the marker-pair pivot still carries the original
heading-based body schema with no marker pairs anywhere. The parser
validates the frontmatter, walks the body looking for required
marker pairs, finds none of the twelve markers, and returns:

```
{
  kind: "malformed-body",
  reason: "missing markers: <!-- aide-orientation-start -->,
    <!-- aide-orientation-end -->, <!-- aide-config-start -->,
    <!-- aide-config-end -->, <!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->,
    <!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->,
    <!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->,
    <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->,
    <!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->,
    <!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->,
    <!-- aide-research-index-start -->,
    <!-- aide-research-index-end -->"
}
```

There is no transitional read path: the parser does not auto-detect
the heading-based schema, does not auto-rewrite, does not silently
default the missing sections.

**A pre-rework four-pair `brain.aide` returns malformed-body naming
the missing new markers and the retired-named ones.** A user whose
`brain.aide` was scaffolded under the prior four-pair schema has a
body with `aide-prose-*`, `aide-playbook-*`, `aide-study-playbook-*`,
and `aide-research-*` pairs in the right relative order — every pair
present, but three of them named with the retired vocabulary and
two pairs the new schema requires (`aide-config-*`,
`aide-update-playbook-*`) entirely absent. The parser walks the
body, finds no `aide-orientation-*` pair, no `aide-config-*` pair,
no `aide-playbook-index-*` pair, no `aide-update-playbook-*` pair,
and no `aide-research-index-*` pair — every required marker is
missing because the pre-rework names are not the new names — and
returns:

```
{
  kind: "malformed-body",
  reason: "missing markers: <!-- aide-orientation-start -->,
    <!-- aide-orientation-end -->, <!-- aide-config-start -->,
    <!-- aide-config-end -->, <!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->,
    <!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->,
    <!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->,
    <!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->,
    <!-- aide-research-index-start -->,
    <!-- aide-research-index-end -->"
}
```

(The `aide-study-playbook-*` pair carries through the rework
unchanged, so it is the only marker the user already has by the
correct name.) The user hand-edits their file: rename
`aide-prose-*` to `aide-orientation-*`, rename `aide-playbook-*` to
`aide-playbook-index-*`, rename `aide-research-*` to
`aide-research-index-*`, and paste in the new `aide-config-*` and
`aide-update-playbook-*` sections in the correct positions. No
`aide_upgrade` carve-out auto-injects defaults; no transitional
shim translates `aide-prose-*` to `aide-orientation-*` silently;
the strict failure is the forcing event that makes the user the
author of every byte that lands in their file post-rework.

**A `brain.aide` with one missing marker pair returns malformed-body
naming the missing pair.** A user adds five of the six required
pairs but forgets the `aide-update-playbook-*` pair. The parser
walks the body, finds five of six pairs, and returns:

```
{
  kind: "malformed-body",
  reason: "missing markers: <!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->,
    <!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->"
}
```

The user adds the missing pair and reruns. The same shape applies
for any single missing pair among the six.

**A `brain.aide` whose markers appear out of order returns
malformed-body naming the order violation.** A user reordered the
sections so `update-playbook` came before `study-playbook`. The
parser detects that the `aide-update-playbook-start` opener appears
before the `aide-study-playbook-start` opener and returns:

```
{
  kind: "malformed-body",
  reason: "marker order violation:
    <!-- aide-update-playbook-start --> appeared before
    <!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->"
}
```

The required order is `orientation` → `config` → `playbook-index` →
`study-playbook` → `update-playbook` → `research-index`; no
exceptions, no auto-reorder.

## Bad examples

**A parser that case-folds or otherwise tolerates marker variants.**
The user writes `<!-- AIDE-ORIENTATION-START -->` (uppercase),
`<!-- Aide-config-start -->` (mixed case),
`<!--aide-playbook-index-start-->` (no surrounding spaces), or
`<!-- aide-update-playbook-start  -->` (extra internal whitespace)
and the parser accepts them all by lowercasing, trimming, or
matching loosely. Within a few releases every `brain.aide` in the
wild has a different marker shape, the install service's scaffold
disagrees with what the parser will accept, and a strict-mode flag
is added later "for discipline" while every existing file is
grandfathered. The parser must match the literal lowercase tokens
(`aide-orientation`, `aide-config`, `aide-playbook-index`,
`aide-study-playbook`, `aide-update-playbook`,
`aide-research-index`) with the precise shape `<!-- <token>-start
-->` / `<!-- <token>-end -->` exactly; any deviation is
`malformed-body` naming the offending marker.

**A parser that auto-renames retired markers to their new
successors.** "Existing users have files using the pre-rework
four-pair body schema; the parser should detect `aide-prose-*` and
silently translate it to `aide-orientation-*`, detect
`aide-playbook-*` and translate it to `aide-playbook-index-*`,
detect `aide-research-*` and translate it to
`aide-research-index-*` — that way they don't have to hand-edit
their file." Now the parser carries a translation table for retired
marker names, every consumer's tests must check both the new names
and the silent translation paths, the install scaffold writes one
shape while half the wild files use the other, and deprecation
never lands because there is no forcing event. Worse: the parser
has become the place that mutates user-owned bytes — even though
the bytes themselves are unchanged on disk, the parsed result the
package treats as authoritative diverges from what the user wrote,
which means `aide_brain` returns content under section names the
user never typed and the install service seeds artifacts whose
provenance the user can't trace back to a marker they wrote. Strict
failure is the only shape that keeps the user's bytes the
authoritative bytes.

**A parser that auto-injects the missing `aide-config-*` or
`aide-update-playbook-*` markers.** The user's `brain.aide` carries
a four-pair body — every prior-schema pair present in the right
relative order — and the parser, "being helpful," detects the
missing `aide-config-*` and `aide-update-playbook-*` pairs and
substitutes a package-side default for each before returning. The
runtime brain tool now serves `aide-config-*` bytes the user never
authored when the config flow runs; the install service seeds an
`update-playbook.md` file in the brain whose bytes came from a
package constant rather than the user's editor. This is exactly
the package-as-vote anti-pattern the move-of-template-content-
out-of-inline-constants was meant to delete: the package gets to
fill in content the user is supposed to own, the user's
`brain.aide` lies about what the agent will actually see, and the
two new sections — which exist precisely so per-integration
orchestration is hand-written per backend — get a uniform default
the package authored. Strict failure is the only shape that keeps
the user authoritative.

**A parser that accepts heading-based input as a transitional read
path.** "Existing users have files using the pre-pivot heading-based
body schema; the parser should accept either the old heading-based
shape OR the new marker-pair shape until adoption stabilizes." Now
the parser carries two parallel grammars, each with its own
validation rules and edge cases; the install scaffold writes one
shape while half the wild files use the other; deprecation never
lands because there is no forcing event. The strict-failure
migration path is the only shape that produces a forcing event:
pre-pivot files return `malformed-body` naming the missing markers,
the user hand-edits their file once, drift disappears.

**A consumer reading another consumer's section across the
live-vs-seed boundary.** `aide_brain` reads `result.playbookIndex`
"to surface a preview of the playbook index in the runtime
briefing" so the agent gets the playbook index without having to
follow the orientation's pointer to the on-disk artifact. The brain
provisioner sneaks a read of `result.orientation` "to validate the
agent-facing prose mentions every entry-point artifact" before
scaffolding. The boot reporter imports `result.config` "to confirm
the config section is non-empty" as a sanity check. Each of these
violates the live-vs-seed split: `aide_brain` reading a seed
section creates a second authoritative source for content that
post-install lives on disk; `provisionBrain` reading a live section
treats agent-facing runtime content as install seed material; the
boot reporter's job is drift detection on `.mcp.json`, not
sanity-checking body sections it doesn't own. The runtime brain
tool reads `orientation` and `config` only. The brain provisioner
reads `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and
`researchIndex` only. The sync verb and boot reporter read
frontmatter only.

**Adding `kind: "update-playbook"` (or any other seed kind) to
`aide_brain`.** A maintainer notices that the
`/aide:update-playbook` shipped command would be slightly simpler
if it could call `aide_brain({ kind: "update-playbook" })` and get
the maintenance methodology back live, instead of going through the
on-disk artifact at `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`. They add
the `update-playbook` kind to the runtime tool's enum and wire it
to return `result.updatePlaybook`. Now the agent has two
authoritative sources for the maintenance methodology — the
brain.aide section AND the on-disk artifact — and any user edit to
either one disagrees with the other. After install, the user's
edits land in the brain file (the live source); the brain.aide
seed section bytes go stale immediately and the runtime tool now
returns stale bytes whenever it's called. The same regression in
different clothing applies to `kind: "playbook-index"`,
`kind: "study-playbook"`, and `kind: "research-index"` — each is a
seed section whose post-install bytes live on disk, and each must
not be reachable through `aide_brain`. The runtime tool's `kind`
enum is exactly `"orientation"` and `"config"`, full stop.

**A parser that returns the body as a single freeform string.**
"The consumers can extract sections themselves with a regex; the
parser shouldn't be in the marker-pair-splitting business." Now
every consumer of `brain.aide` carries its own copy of the body
grammar — six marker pairs to find, the required order to validate,
the nesting and unmatched-marker cases to detect — and the parser
has decentralized the format it exists to centralize. The first
consumer that miscounts a marker boundary scaffolds an entry-point
artifact whose bytes start mid-section; the second consumer that
uses a different regex disagrees about which bytes belong to
`orientation` versus `config`; the parser pretends the body is
opaque while every consumer pretends the body is structured.

**Module-private type definitions for the parsed shape.** The parser
declares its own `interface ParsedBrainAide { ... }` inside
`src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` and exports it for consumers
to import. Now the parsed shape is defined twice, type errors no
longer propagate cleanly because the central type surface is
fragmented, and the `BrainAideConfig` type that already lives in
`src/types/index.ts` sits next to a parallel definition no one
knows is canonical. The parsed-shape fields for the six body
sections (`orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) live in `src/types/index.ts` and
every consumer imports from there.

**Retired field names showing up in the typed result or the parser
code as if still part of the schema.** A maintainer extends the
`ParseBrainAideResult.ok` branch with a `prose` field "for the
agent-facing briefing" instead of `orientation`; a `playbook` field
"for the coding playbook entry-point bytes" instead of
`playbookIndex`; a `research` field "for the research entry-point
bytes" instead of `researchIndex`. These are the retired vocabulary
of the prior schemas — `prose` was renamed to `orientation`,
`playbook` to `playbookIndex`, `research` to `researchIndex` — and
their reappearance in the typed result restores the very names this
rework retired. Every downstream consumer that imports the type
ends up reading from the wrong key; the install service seeds files
under field names that don't match the marker tokens; the
storage-agnostic vocabulary commitment breaks because the safe-list
of body-field names is exactly the new six, not the retired three.
The Hub-suffix variant (`playbookIndexHub`, `studyPlaybookHub`,
`updatePlaybookHub`, `researchIndexHub`, `orientationHub`,
`configHub`) is the same regression in different clothing — the
typed keys are the kebab-to-camel of the marker base names, full
stop.

**A parser that branches on `name`.** The parser reads
`result.name === "some-backend"` and applies different validation
rules for `mcpServerConfig.args` (one backend expects a path arg,
another expects an API token arg, etc.) — or worse, applies
different section-extraction rules for the body ("backend X
requires a seventh marker pair, others don't"). This re-introduces
the identity-inference anti-pattern the brainBackends retirement
was specifically meant to delete — different brains may share
`name` values, and the package never branches on the label. If a
future user wants brain-specific behavior, they encode it in their
hand-written `config` section (where the integration's wiring flow
already lives), not in a registry the package owns.

**A parser that silently strips deprecated fields.** The user's
old `brain.aide` still carries `connector: some-backend`,
`rootPath: D:/brains/my-brain`, and `tools.read`. The parser sees
them, drops them on the floor, and returns a config containing
only `name` and `mcpServerConfig`. The user believes their file is
honored — their `tools.read` declaration is sitting right there in
the file — but nothing in the package reads it. Months later they
wonder why changing `tools.read` has no effect. The parser must
reject deprecated fields by name.

**A parser that introspects `mcpServerConfig.args` to derive a
`rootPath`.** "We removed the `rootPath` field, but we still need
to know the brain root path for the boot reporter, so let's just
take `args[1]` as the rootPath." Path validation is the launcher's
job at MCP server startup; the package does not second-guess it.
Reading args for any purpose other than passing them through to
`.mcp.json` is the same regression as a `rootPath` field in
different clothing — the parser stays a pure passthrough that
preserves the typed `(string | null)[]` shape and lets every
consumer read the slice it owns from the typed result.

**A parser that pattern-matches `<BRAIN_PATH>` (or any other
literal-string sentinel) as a parse-level signal.** "We need a way
to tell that the brain is unwired, so let's detect `<BRAIN_PATH>`,
`<API_TOKEN>`, `__UNWIRED__`, or any angle-bracketed string in
`mcpServerConfig.args` and surface a special unwired-state to the
boot reporter / sync verb / `aide_brain` config flow." This is the
retired anti-pattern this rework deletes: a literal sentinel is a
string that passes string-equality checks against itself
identically to how a real value passes string-equality checks
against itself, which is exactly the byte-for-byte-agreement bug
that let `<BRAIN_PATH>` round-trip through drift checks unchanged
while every consumer disagreed about whether the slot was wired.
The unwired-slot signal is YAML null — a structural type the YAML,
JS, and TypeScript layers all recognize as distinct from every
string — and the parser's contract is to preserve that structure
verbatim. No special-case branch detects sentinel-shaped strings,
no helper expands them, no consumer reads them as state. Every
string in args is just a string; every null in args is the
unwired-slot signal.

**A parser that coerces null entries in args to empty strings.**
"YAML null is awkward to type-check downstream — let's just turn
nulls into empty strings at the parse boundary so the rest of the
stack sees `string[]` and doesn't have to handle null." Now sync's
null-refusal precondition never fires (it sees an empty string at
the unwired index, not a null), `buildBrainState`'s null-detection
precedence rule is dead (there are no nulls to precedence-detect),
the `/aide:brain config` flow has no structural way to identify
which arg position the user has not yet wired (every arg is a
string, including the unwired ones), and `.mcp.json` lands with
empty-string args the launcher silently fails on. The
`(string | null)[]` typing is load-bearing precisely because every
downstream branching decision depends on null being preserved as a
distinct type at the parse boundary.

**A parser that drops null entries from `args` or compacts the
array.** "Null entries probably shouldn't be in the args anyway —
let's filter them out so consumers see only real string args."
Now index positions in the parsed result no longer match index
positions in the source YAML. A `/aide:brain config` flow whose
prose says "edit `args[3]` to replace null with the absolute path"
edits the wrong index, because the parser silently shifted every
post-null entry up by one. `buildBrainState`'s drift-check
disagrees with what the user actually wrote, because the parser is
showing the boot reporter a 3-element array while the YAML has 4.
The verbatim invariant generalizes to array shape — null entries
preserve their indexes one-to-one with source YAML, full stop.

**A parser that rejects null entries in args as
`malformed-frontmatter`.** "Null in args looks malformed — let's
treat it as a frontmatter validation failure so users see a clear
error." But null is part of the args type contract — the explicit
unwired-slot signal that downstream consumers branch on. A
`brain.aide` whose `args` array carries null at one or more
indexes is well-formed and parses to a well-formed
`BrainAideConfig`. Treating null as malformed at the parse layer
would force users to scaffold an unwired `brain.aide` with no
path-shaped slot at all, breaking the contract that the parser is
the single canonical reader and that nulls flow through to
consumers untouched. The cold-install default — null at the path
slot — would itself fail to parse, and `aide_init({ category:
"brain" })` would land a file the parser immediately rejects.

**A parser that injects default values for null entries in args.**
"If `args[3]` is null, let's substitute a sensible default
absolute path — the user's home directory, the current working
directory, or a hardcoded brain root — so the launcher actually
works." This is the package-as-vote anti-pattern in its purest
form: the parser overrides the user's explicit "I have not wired
this yet" signal with bytes the package authored. The
`/aide:brain config` flow loses its forcing event (the user's
brain.aide claims to be unwired, but `.mcp.json` carries a value
the user never authorized), the user's edit is no longer
authoritative (their null is silently overwritten on every parse),
and the unwired-slot warning surface goes silent because there are
no nulls left to detect. Null is preserved verbatim; package-side
defaults never replace user-authored structure.

**A parser that adds an `entryFile` field back to the schema "for
documentation."** Someone notices the orientation section keeps
mentioning the entry-point artifacts and proposes a frontmatter
`entryFile` field "so it's structured." No code reads the field;
the agent still picks up the artifact paths from the orientation
section. This is validation theater — a frontmatter field whose
only consumer is the parser's own validation. The schema must
justify each field by naming a real runtime consumer. Anything
else belongs in the body sections or nowhere.

**A parser that interpolates any body section.** Before returning
results, the parser walks `orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, or `researchIndex` and replaces
`${name}` occurrences with the resolved label. The first user
whose `config` section legitimately documents a `${variable}`
syntax has their wiring flow mangled; the first `studyPlaybook`
section that legitimately contains a `${...}` template example gets
rewritten before reaching disk. Worse: every section author now
needs to know which placeholders the package will rewrite before
delivery, and the temptation to "just template the body sections
too" lands. Substitution lives only in `mcpServerConfig.args`;
every body section returns verbatim.

**A parser that throws on missing brain.aide.** The host project
calls the parser, no file exists, the parser raises an exception,
the consumer's MCP-protocol response is an error with no
`instructions` field. Missing-file is a normal pre-init state; it
must be a tagged-result variant.

**A parser that injects defaults for missing frontmatter fields.**
The user's `brain.aide` is missing `name`. The parser silently
defaults it to a hardcoded label. Required fields are required, and
a missing required field is a structured failure the user resolves
by editing.

**A consumer that re-reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` directly
instead of calling this parser.** The boot reporter implements its
own YAML read because "we only need `mcpServerConfig` to compare
against `.mcp.json`, the rest is ceremony." Two consumers now
decode the file format, and the moment the format gains a new
section, a new required frontmatter field, or rejects a deprecated
one, they disagree on what's valid. This rework's six-section
schema doubled the number of marker pairs, which doubles the
surface area where two parsers can disagree — exactly why
centralization is load-bearing.

**A parser that tolerates nested marker pairs.** The user's
`brain.aide` has an `<!-- aide-config-start -->` opener appearing
between the `<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-orientation-end -->` markers — the config section is
nested inside the orientation section. Or an
`<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->` opener appears between the
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` and
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` markers, nesting update-playbook
inside study-playbook (a tempting "the maintenance methodology
belongs with the navigation methodology" containment). The parser,
walking with a forgiving stack-based grammar, successfully
extracts the sections by treating each inner pair as a nested
region. Now the user has invented a containment hierarchy the
schema does not have — the live-vs-seed split is supposed to be
flat: two live siblings, four seed siblings, no parent-child.
Any nesting of markers inside another marker pair is
`malformed-body` naming the offending nested marker.

**A parser that recovers from an opener without a closer.** The
user's `brain.aide` has `<!-- aide-research-index-start -->` but
no `<!-- aide-research-index-end -->` — the file simply ends after
the research-index bytes. The parser, "being helpful," treats
end-of-file as an implicit closer. Every opener requires its
matching closer; an unmatched opener is `malformed-body` naming
the opener.

